{"step_index":56,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-19T19:12:32Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-20T00:42:32+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-20T00:42:36+05:30\nThe search for \"\"UltraFusion\" Apple packaging \"die-to-die\" bandwidth latency\" returned the following summary:\nApple’s **UltraFusion** is a proprietary, high-density packaging architecture designed to interconnect multiple silicon dies, allowing them to function as a single, unified system-on-chip (SoC)[1][2][3]. By utilizing a silicon interposer with over 10,000 signals, UltraFusion provides extremely high bandwidth and low latency, avoiding the traditional performance trade-offs associated with connecting chips via a motherboard[4][5][6].\n\n### Bandwidth and Latency Specifications\nThe performance of UltraFusion has scaled with each generation of Apple silicon:\n\n*   **M1 Ultra, M2 Ultra, and M3 Ultra:** These chips utilize a dual-die configuration connected via UltraFusion, providing **2.5 TB/s** of inter-processor bandwidth[7][5][6].\n*   **M5 Ultra:** Representing a shift to a quad-die architecture, this generation features an upgraded version of UltraFusion that increases inter-die bandwidth to **over 4.4 TB/s** and improves connection density by more than 6x[8][9][10].\n\n### Key Characteristics\n*   **Unified Architecture:** Because of the massive bandwidth and low latency provided by UltraFusion, software perceives the multiple dies as a single, unified processor[2][6]. This eliminates the need for developers to rewrite code to account for the multi-die layout[6].\n*   **High Connection Density:** The technology uses a silicon interposer to bridge the dies with thousands of connections, which Apple claims provides significantly higher connection density than other industry interconnect technologies[7][11].\n*   **Performance Benefits:** By keeping the interconnect within the package, UltraFusion minimizes data transmission latency and reduces the power consumption typically incurred when moving data between separate chips[1][4][12]. This design allows Apple to achieve high-performance levels for demanding professional tasks—such as 3D rendering, video transcoding, and AI computing—while maintaining high power efficiency[1][13].\n\nSources:\n[1] [icdrex.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFwJnA1aq8S4L3SbyWm-dXWEsJnRqGSq84-amDIz8zESy3fbR8IxG2DVfiV6PvnnlMhJ13ovkFLZgtFv-97iGenAF53xVq60bkZ2DfPUnC1SYoIkBSSrNMscmgQR5U9_nnCeQojZJ3OaQlLNCbaAQ3925P93EScOau8Axgst9SAFKZn)\n[2] [techpowerup.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEVT_wv9kawvK7yffiCI9IuHY2TwMXZbAHNl4xnFgrp87FB5mby7eVrw9cNaP1MWodhAGDCjBFcxpcwUiG8uLjuK6L_obHsxOdSqTP_RI_OtuV-fwe-EJmPXc1sin0VzLvWCKbvhRg=)\n[3] [youtube.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHJ_5680Vu1eTf63FrQgf9cMFuZxQWXTMP4SEsHp7ez7E-I3-eMfBSpuV3-E0vms3-XRDiLi2dsUK0tAga3eyss8Ouxhvmp44vULwi1wTFDkRsnh9NGirxpWRccMsivpkho)\n[4] [hothardware.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFYUnj_kKeMU2KOcJfrBdSvscQmTnMbTo5_HNCPWpf3WjviSC0hSZfS7kLt6KcAZx1I2fvwFnt5vE1zC8fZGPolLohyaV6MMEZGhgWPwtgxtGxQ7pWNhtZzmakpY9Tv_2DupJTGhiisnz-baMjne4Ze6OOKiTGl0e8_U03Mz49b2v_rfC5RPpZ7dLsd7g==)\n[5] [apple.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGWnbNKCLMCvntAqYrbkOWbmeow6F658L4DpwXcIzAzSpK5_HaI5s5ryiT-CuO4l-jWZK4yb57dKbFKyIGAHMSWWKoZeuwnG1xO7-eXIR04EOdAGIhBZ20ucvwXn2DRCchgTqQDZ4l5d5G5fqNKrmIoZXX1obG_rl9PoW6bkKUMD298ZE3eCdyEg_KNIXEwa2mQQfpfH0sHHDjtKrlG)\n[6] [apple.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGFDws8Ep1xG_2eeD1xo1MzV5T1vjbNJmBNEfzAFB1Z4UXAgodkuhpqdIU9qJlvxzqGeUlXXpprlakphIRseiNNh_SOEHsOm3BeZ81mr4U0lO-kEpN6otlQXgZ9-oTWFgqeD8Qvr1lo4wZXaYRBGbr4cav1ulDY1q-YxQ==)\n[7] [computerworld.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFclYzdrRUaVJx1U7GGBXFHayYHXPvQUS4Uez1Gpln_n87p_zg6otDx-YxR_UnimDGcST