{"step_index":61,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-19T19:13:05Z","content":"I have completed the deep research investigation into official and legitimate methods for sharing RAM and compute resources between a Mac Mini and a MacBook Pro, and I have transmitted the full report to the parent agent via `send_message`.\n\n### Summary of Core Findings:\n1. **Direct RAM Pooling (Unified Memory):** Physically, architecturally, and mathematically impossible over external cables. Unified Memory Architecture (UMA) is hard-wired directly into the SoC with 100–819+ GB/s bandwidth and 10–20 ns latency. Thunderbolt 4/5 cannot bridge cache coherency across motherboards due to a 500x bandwidth deficit and 1,000x latency penalty.\n2. **macOS System Architecture (Sequoia & beyond):** Virtual memory (`dynamic_pager`) strictly pages to local NVMe SSDs; network swap does not exist in macOS.\n3. **Continuity Features (Universal Control, Sidecar, AirPlay):** These operate at the input/display streaming layers only; zero compute or RAM is pooled.\n4. **Official Distributed Workloads:** \n   - **Apple Compressor:** Native \"Shared Computers\" distributed batch transcoding.\n   - **Apple MLX:** Distributed training/inference across multiple Macs via Thunderbolt RDMA (`JACCL`).\n   - **Xgrid & Logic Node:** Deprecated/discontinued.\n5. **Operational Workload Offloading:** Fully legitimate and supported via **High Performance Screen Sharing** (Apple Silicon to Apple Silicon over Thunderbolt Bridge with 4K 60fps 4:4:4 color), **SSH / VS Code Remote**, and headless local AI servers (e.g., Ollama/MLX-LM)."}
